## ABSTRACT
We for the first time rationally design CNTs twinned MOFs-derived porous carbon sponges (CNTs/MOFs-C) through an electrostatic interaction between positively charged MOFs-C and negatively charged CNTs, novel \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) hydroxyhalide nanoparticle with hydroxyl functional groups and polar bonds is in-situ incorporated. The electrochemical performance of lithium-sulfur batteries (LSBs) based on CNTs/MOFs-C/Al<sub>2</sub>(OH)<sub>2.76</sub>F<sub>3.24</sub>/S cathode can be superior to reported LSBs performance, showing a specific capacity of 889 mA h g<sup>-1</sup> at 300th cycle at current density of 500 mA g<sup>-1</sup>. Notably, a longest cycling life span of 2000 cycles at high current density of 2 A g<sup>-1</sup> with a specific capacity of 719 mA h g<sup>-1</sup> maintained and coulombic efficiency of approximately 100% based on CNTs/MOFs-C/Al<sub>2</sub>(OH)<sub>2.76</sub>F<sub>3.24</sub>/S cathode is reported. Coupling of CNTs with MOFs-C to form 3D interconnected network can provide an efficient pathway for fast ion delivery and electron transport, effectively enhances charge transfer kinetics. The in-situ formation of \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) polar nanoparticles further immobilize sulfur species by strong chemical interactions between the intermediate
polysulfides and hydroxyl functional groups, Al-F polar bonds The rational design route for hydroxyhalide nanoparticle anchored MOFs-derived porous carbon sponges interlinked by CNTs is novel and inspiring, opening up a new gate for sulfur hosts in LSBs.
Keywords Lithium-sulfur batteries; carbon nanotubes; metal-organic framework; hydroxyl group; chemical immobilization
## Introduction
Compared with traditional lithium ion batteries, the high abundance and environmental benignity of elemental sulfur, especially the theoretical energy density of \(2500\ \mathrm{W\ h\ kg^{-1}}\), about 3-5 times higher than that of traditional Li-ion battery, [1] make lithium-sulfur batteries (LSBs) become one of the most promising candidates to satisfy the increased requirement for high performance energy storage devices. [2, 3] Even so, the potential commercial application of LSBs is greatly hindered by a variety of challenges that mainly arise from the insulated nature of sulfur and lithium sulfides, the high solubility of the polysulfides in electrolyte, and remarkable volume change (79%) during cycling process, [4] leading to severe shuttle effect, low coulombic efficiency and pronounced capacity fading on cycling. [5] Furthermore, the dissolution of intermediate polysulfide may lead unfavorable reaction of the polysulfides with the Li anode to form insulated \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) layer, causing significant polarization. [6-8]
Recently, porous carbon materials are proven to be excellent sulfur immobilizer as cathodes for enhanced electrochemical performance LSBs, due to the facilitated charge transfer ability and the tunable porous structures. [9] In order to avoid the unfavorable formation of polysulfides, small sulfur molecules of \(\mathrm{S}_{2-4}\) are designed to be confined within the micro-porous space in carbon nanotubes by Guo's group. [10] In fact, when \(\mathrm{S}_{8}\) is used as active material, the weak physical adsorption effect provided by the nonpolar carbon matrix could not efficiently suppress the dissolution of polysulfide intermediates. To provide polar sites for chemically absorbing the polysulfide intermediates during electrochemical process, oxides, [11, 12] sulfides, [13] hydroxide, [14, 15] and nitrides [16] have been proposed as cathode host for sulfur specie chemical immobilizer. Polar hosts can effectively adsorb polysulfide intermediates based on strong chemical interaction between host and polysulfides, showing greatly improved electrochemical performance LSBs. [17] Nazar's group reported the delaminated 2D conductive MXene nanosheets as cathode hosts to improve the performance of LSBs by chemical interaction and polar absorption effects. [18] It is revealed that intermediate polysulfide products are immobilized effectively due to the formation of thiosulfate between the polysulfides and hydroxyl functional group on the surface of the MXene.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) derived porous carbon materials, display a typically exceptional specific surface area and porous structure. [19] Furthermore, the existence of metal species can promote the fundamental interactions between polysulfides and the host framework during the electrochemical process, [20] thus providing a unique opportunity to develop a novel kind of highly tailorable carbon materials with polar sites. [21-24] Lou et al designs ZIF-67 derived double-shelled nanocages with two shells of cobalt hydroxide and layered double hydroxides as a sulfur host for LSBs. [25, 26] However, the electrochemical charge transfer kinetics for the MOFs-derived porous electrodes is still significantly limited owing to the poor electron conductivity due to low graphitization degree and poor contact between MOF-derived particles. In contrast, carbon nanotubes (CNTs) with highly extended one-dimensional (1D) graphitic structure are capable of offering favorable long-range electrical conductivity within the electrode. [27-29] Primarily, the 1D geometry of CNTs could enhance charge flux at the interface between active material and the electrolyte due to the short ion diffusion distance and the sufficient contact area with the electrolyte. [30-32] Furthermore, 1D CNTs can be expected to effectively interconnect MOFs-derived porous carbon to form 3D framework to achieve excellent electrochemical performance due to the rapid charge transport from CNTs to MOFs-derived porous carbon. [33-35] Up to now, it is of greatly fundamental importance and challenge to rationally design 3D interconnected porous architecture LSBs cathodes combing with the advantages of 3D MOFs-derived porous carbon and 1D CNTs based on simultaneously physical and chemical immobilizing effect for sulfur species. Notably, the introduction of polarized hydroxyhalide nanoparticles with strong chemical immobilizing effect should be more efficient to further improve electrochemical performance of LSBs.
Herein, we for the first time rafionally design CNTs twinned MOFs-derived porous carbon sponges (CNTs/MOFs-C) through an electrostatic interaction between the positively charged MOFs-C and negatively charged CNTs, a novel kind of \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) hydroxyhalide nanoparticle with hydroxyl functional groups and polar bonds is introduced to further enhance chemically immobilize sulfur species. Porous CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S hybrids exhibit excellent electrochemical performance. \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) particles can efficiently immobilize sulfur species by chemical interactions due to the hydroxyl functional groups and Al-F polar bonds. Highly conductive CNTs twined on MOFs-C construct electron conductive framework. The porous carbon sponges, CNTs and \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) particles firmly confine the sulfur species synergistically, thus leading to greatly suppressed shuttle effect and low polarization. The rational design route for hydroxyhalide nanoparticle anchored MOFs-derived porous carbon sponges interlinked by CNTs is novel and inspiring, opening up a new gate for sulfur hosts in LSBs.
## Experimental section
## Preparation of CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S
MOFs-derived carbon sponge is denoted as MOFs-C, MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)O\(_3\) was obtained by annealing Al-MOF [19] at \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5\,\mathrm{h}\) under Ar. Further treatment with appropriate amount HF (aq. \(10\,\mathrm{wt\%}\), 4g) for 12h makes \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) \((\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) 1g, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \(200\mathrm{ml}\)) transform to \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\cdot\mathrm{xH}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), then the \(\mathrm{xH}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was easily removed during heat treatment, finally transforms to \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\), and MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\) was obtained. In the present work, \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) was obtained due to the chemical equilibrium between HF (weak acid) and \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) (amphoteric metal oxide), which is interesting. The MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\) was modified with poly (diallyl dimethyl ammonium chloride) (2.149g PDDA 35% solution, 0.363g tris, 0.173g NaCl), the CNTs were treated with concentrated sulfuric acid (140 \({}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), 6h hydrothermal). After \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) with stirring, CNTs were twined on MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\). The mixture of the well mixed host materials and sulfur (mass ratio 1:5) is sealed in a glass tube under vacuum and then heated at \(155\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). Therefore sulfur was impregnated into the pores of the host, CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S was obtained. For a compassion study, MOFs-C/S, CNTs/MOFs-C/S, CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S were used as cathodes for LSBs.
## Material Characterizations
XRD patterns were collected using a Rigaku D/Max-Rb diffractometer equipped with \(\mathrm{Cu}\,\mathrm{K}\alpha\) radiation \((\lambda=1.5406\,\mathrm{\AA})\). The morphology of the synthesized products was analyzed using SU-70 field emission scanning electron microscopy. The microstructures of the synthesized products were analyzed using JEM-2100 high-resolution transmission electron microscopy at an acceleration voltage of \(200\,\mathrm{kV}\). Nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherms were determined at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) using BJ builder Kubo X1000. Raman spectra were measured and collected using a \(632.8\,\mathrm{nm}\) laser with a JY HR800 under ambient conditions, with a laser spot size of about \(1\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy characterization was carried out using an ESCALAB 250 instrument with a \(150\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{Al}\,\mathrm{K}\alpha\) probe beam.
## Electrochemical measurements
Electrochemical measurements were carried out using 2025 coin-type cells, in which the MOFs-C/S, CNTs/MOFs-C/S, CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S hybrids were used as the working electrodes, a lithium metal foil was used as reference electrode, a Celgard 2325 as the separator, and a solution of \(1.0\,\mathrm{M}\) LiTFSI in mixed DOL and DME (1:1 by volume) with \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) additives as the electrolyte (70 uL per cell). The MOFs-C/S, CNTs/MOFs-C/S, CNT/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S hybrid slurry was made by mixing \(70\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) active material, \(15\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) LA132 (Indigo, China) binder and \(15\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) ethylene carbonate in deionized water. After stirring for \(10\,\mathrm{h}\), the
slurry was spread onto \(20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) aluminum foil, which was followed by drying treatment at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under vacuum overnight. The dried electrode was then punched into round discs with sulfur loading of 3.4, 3.3 and \(3.1~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}\). Fresh coin cells were assembled in an Ar-filled glove box. The cells were discharged and charged on a LAND CT2001A battery test system from \(2.8~\mathrm{V}\) to \(1.6~\mathrm{V}\) at \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Cyclic voltammetry and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy measurements were carried out using an electrochemical workstation (PARSTAT 2273) at room temperature. [23] The electrical conductivity of the sample was tested using RTS-5 4-point probes resistivity measurement system.
## Results and discussion
Scheme 1 illustrates the designed synthetic route for CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S hybrids and the sulfur immobilization mechanism. The MOFs-derived porous carbon sponges (MOFs-C) with \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) nanoparticles anchored are obtained from the Al-MOF precursor under annealing treatment at \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in Ar atmosphere, and then treated with HF.[36] In order to couple 3D MOFs-derived C with 1D CNTs, the 3D MOFs-C is modified to be positively charged by PDDA, and 1D CNTs are treated by concentrated sulfuric acid to be negatively charged. PDDA is a kind of cationic surfactant; the MOFs-derived carbon is functionalized to have positive surface charges. CNTs functionalized by concentrated \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) are negatively charged, and hydroxyl and carboxyl groups are connected on the surface of CNTs. The electrostatic interaction between the positively charged MOFs-derived carbon and negatively charged CNTs enables the two carbon components self-assembly. After sulfur species are immobilized into the pores of the carbon matrix, a hierarchically 3D CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S hybrid is obtained with CNTs twined between the MOFs-derived C, with \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) particles homogeneously embedded.
The 3D hierarchical hybrids with 3D interconnected charge transfer pathways and interlinked electrolyte diffusion channels could achieve excellent performance for LSBS, due to the synergistic effects of the merits of 1D CNTs and MOFs-derived porous carbon sponges, so this unique structure could enable rapid charge transport from CNTs to MOFs-C. More importantly, a significantly chemical immobilizing effect is imposed on the polysulfide intermediates arisen from chemical interaction between \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) and lithium polysulfide, as indicated by the schematic in the red rectangle at left down side in Schema 1. Due to the presence of hydroxyl groups on surface of \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\), the thiosulfate groups are formed by oxidation of lithium polysulfide species, as Nazar's report revealed,[37] the generated thiosulfate groups are able to anchor newly generated polysulfides to form polythionates, thus preventing dissolution of ploysulfide into electrolyte. Furthermore, the polysulfides can also be immobilized through chemical reaction between Al and S species due to the presence of strong polar Al-F bonding in \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\). More information and evidence are given in the following discussion (IR spectra in Fig. 2d and XPS spectra in Fig. 4).
The microstructures of 3D CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S hybrids are depicted in Fig. 1. Fig. 1a shows a typical FESEM image of CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S architectures, clearly showing CNTs are homogeneously twinned with MOFs-derived carbon sponges, showing a diameter of \(30\;\mathrm{nm}\) and \(400\;\mathrm{nm}\) for CNTs and MOFs-C sponges, respectively. Further information on the morphologies of the CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S hybrids can be revealed in FESEM image in Fig. S1. (Fig. S2 shows SEM image and TEM image of MOFs-C). As clearly revealed in typical TEM images (Fig. 1b) of CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S, the 3D MOFs-C is composed of porous carbon layers, showing abundant mesopores between carbon layer and micropores within carbon layer (Fig. S3-S4), which could provide enough space for high content of sulfur loading. From TEM image in Fig. 1b, it is clearly shown that CNTs are uniformly twinned with 3D MOFs-C. Coupling of CNTs with MOFs-C to form the 3D interconnected electronic network (Fig. S5) can provide an efficient pathway for fast ion delivery and electron transport effectively enhances charge
transfer kinetics. [31, 38] High magnification TEM images in Fig. 1c and Fig. S6 show that CNTs are successfully coupled with MOFs-C through an electrostatic interaction between the positively charged MOFs-C and negatively charged CNTs. The diffraction rings in electron diffraction pattern (Fig. 1d) for CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24 match well with that of (111), (311), (400), (440) plane of \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\). A high magnification TEM image (Fig. 1e) illustrates that highly crystalline \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) nanoparticles are anchored on MOFs-C matrix. The inset of left upper side gives a typical lattice image of single \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) nanoparticle, the highlighted d-spacing corresponding well to that of (111) and (1-11) planes of \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\). A EDS spectrum from the CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S hybrid in Fig. 1f suggests that the hybrid is composed of C, S, Al, O, and F elements. A strong peak for S elemental can be observed due to the high amount of sulfur species. EDS mapping images in Fig. S7 clearly show that C, S, Al, O and F elements are homogenously distributed along the CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S hybrid.
XRD patterns in Fig. 2a and Fig. S8 show the phase components of the obtained samples. For the XRD pattern of the MOFs-derived carbon sponges (MOFs-C) and CNTs/MOFs-C hybrids, only one peak at about \(22^{\circ}\) corresponding to (002) of carbon is observed. The presence of the \(\mathrm{Al}_{x}(\mathrm{OH})_{y}\mathrm{F}_{z}\) can be determined by the XRD and TEM results. As shown in Figure 2a, some XRD peaks of the as-prepared products match well with that of standard PDF card of \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) (PDF. No: 41-0381). Besides, the diffraction rings of electron diffraction pattern in Figure 1d for CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24 correspond to the (111), (311), (400), (440) plane of \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\). The XRD and TEM results suggest that the \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) is successfully anchored on the MOFs-C. After sulfur impregnation, the peaks for sulfur are observed clearly for MOFs-C/S, CNTs/MOFs-C/S, and CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S hybrids, corresponding to a high sulfur loading content. The TGA test (Fig. 2b) confirms the sulfur loading content is 79, 76 and \(72\ \mathrm{\wt\%}\) for MOFs-C/S, CNTs/MOFs-C/S, and CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S hybrids, respectively. The high loading content of S can be attributed to the large surface area and abundantly porous structure of the MOF-derived C sponges and CNTs (see following BET analysis). In Raman spectra of the MOF-derived C, CNTs/MOFs-C, CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24 samples (Fig. 2c), two strong peaks at 1322 and \(1570~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) can be assigned to D band and G band of the MOFs-derived carbon sponges, revealing the partially graphitized nature of the MOF-derived porous carbon sponges and CNTs.[26, 27] After sulfur impregnation, no Raman peaks for sulfur is observed, illustrating that sulfur may occupy the porous sites of matrixes rather than on the surface. The infrared spectrum (IR) of the CNTs after treatment by concentrated sulfuric acid is shown in Fig. 2d. The absorption peaks at about 3440, 1390, 1630 and \(1050~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) correspond to O-H, C-H, \(\mathbf{C}{=}\mathbf{O}\) and C-C, respectively.[41, 42] For the \(\mathrm{MOFs-C/Al_{2}(O H)_{2.76}F_{3.24}}\) , another peak corresponding to Al-F bond shaking at about \(620~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is detected. For the CNTs/MOFs- \(\mathrm{C/Al_{2}(O H)_{2.76}F_{3.24}/S}\) cathode cycled for 5 cycles, the cell is disassembled at discharged state, and a strong peak at about \(1140~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) for \(\scriptstyle\mathbf{S}=\mathbf{O}\) bonding appears.[43] It is deduced that the thiosulfate or polythionate complexes are formed due to the chemical interaction between sulfur and hydroxyl group, and sufficient evidence will be described in the XPS results.
From Fig. 3a, it is suggested that the isotherms of the MOFs-C, CNTs/MOFs-C, CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24 show a similar type IV isotherm with H1 hysteresis loop, indicating the presence of abundant meso-pores. Furthermore, the rapid rise in low relative pressure \((\mathrm{P/0}<0.1)\) corresponds to a high amount of micro-pores. It displays high specific area of 1170, 756 and \(532~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for MOFs-C, CNTs/MOFs-C, CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24, respectively. From the pore size distribution in Fig. 3b, it is indicated that the main peak of the size distribution is at about \(4~\mathrm{nm}\), and the intensity of the peaks for MOFs-C, CNTs/MOFs-C, CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24 shows a gradual decrease trend. Besides, there are also broad peaks at about 5-30
nm, 5-20 nm and 6-20 nm for the MOFs-C, CNTs/MOFs-C, CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\), showing the existence of mesopores in the host materials. The presence of high amount of micro-pores was revealed from the inset of Fig. 3b, showing a pore size distribution at 0.4-0.6 nm. After sulfur impregnation, isotherm in Fig. 3c turns to type II, indicating that most of the pores are occupied by the sulfur species. Compared with Fig. 3b, the weak peak of about 40 nm is observed in Fig. 3d and the peak at 4 nm associated with the meso-pores and 0.4 nm related to micro-pores in the host materials almost disappears, suggesting that the pores are mostly occupied by the incorporated S nanoparticles. The pores that still exist in the sulfur containing cathode materials could buffer the volume change during cycling process. According to the XPS (Fig. S9) and TGA for CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S sample, Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\) is about 6 wt% in CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S composite. To validate the chemical immobilizing effect for sulfur species of the Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\) component, the cycled CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S cathodes were disassembled cautiously at the end of the discharge, and XPS analysis was performed, as shown in Fig. 4. Fig. 4a showed the high-resolution spectrum of S 2p before cycle, the peaks located at 164.3 eV and 165.4 eV could be assigned to S2p 3/2 and S2p 1/2, respectively, indicating that sulfur was impregnated into the pores of matrix in the form of Ss. After 10 cycles, the XPS peaks for S 2p in Fig. 4b showed an obvious shift, indicating the possible formation of sulfides (162.6 eV), [14] thiosulfate (167.0 eV), [37] polythionate complexes (167.8 eV) [44] and sulfate (168.9 and 170.2 eV). [11] The presence of thiosulfate (at 167.0 eV) and polythionate complexes (at 167.8 eV) may originate from strong interaction between polysulfides and the hydroxyl groups during electrochemical process. For the Al 2p spectrum before cycle (Fig. 4c), it could be fitted into three peaks (74.4, 74.8 and 75.4 eV), corresponding to Al-OH (74.4, 74.8 eV) and Al-F (75.4 eV) chemical environment, respectively. [45, 47] After 10 cycles (Fig. 4d), these XPS peaks shift towards lower binding energy (74.3, 74.7 and 75.1 eV), which may result from the strong chemical interaction between Sn\(^2\)+ and Al\(^{3+}\). For Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\), the presence of the Al-OH and Al-F chemical bonds tend to make the surface of Al\(^{3+}\) outward to be Lewis acid, [20] thus leading to the capture of Sn\(^2\)+ by Al\(^{3+}\). [48] The O 1s XPS spectra before cycling and after cycle were shown in (Fig. 4e-4f). For the O1s before cycle (Fig. 4e), peaks for Al-O, O-H, and C=O could be observed. Three peaks after cycle at 531.4, 531.9 and 532.5 eV in the O1s XPS spectra, correspond to the Al-O, O-S and C=O chemical bonding, respectively. Great changes for C=O bonding might be attributed to the vestigial electrolyte on the cathode during XPS experiment. [49, 50] The binding energy of F showed two peaks at 685.3eV and 688.8eV, corresponding to the Al-F and C-F bonding in Fig. S10a. [51, 52] This illustrates that F could not only chemically bond with Al, but also with C. After cycling process, significant decrease of binding energy for Al-F XPS peak took place, shifting to a position of lower binding energy due to the absorption for polysulfides (Fig. S10b). The chemically immobilizing mechanism could be summarized as the effect of hydroxyl groups by formation of thiosulfate surface species, followed by a Lewis acid-base interaction between the Al\(^{3+}\) atoms in Al-F or Al-O polar bonding and polysulfides. [17]
To evaluate the advantages of the designed hybrid cathodes for LSBs, the electrochemical performance of MOFs-C/S, CNTs/MOF-C/S and CNTs/MOF-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S cathodes under current density of 200, 500 and 1000 mA g\(^{-1}\) between 2.8 and 1.6 V are comparatively investigated (Fig. 5). As shown in Fig. 5a, the CNTs/MOF-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S displays the best performance among these three hybrid cathodes. The highest discharge capacities of 1563, 1468 and 1361 mA h g\(^{-1}\) were obtained with 200, 500 and 1000 mA g\(^{-1}\),
corresponding to initial coulombic efficiency of \(97\%\) , \(96\%\) and \(94\%\) , respectively. Subsequently, the CNTs/MOFs- \(\mathrm{C/Al_{2}(O H)_{2.76}F_{3.24}/S}\) displayed a good cycling stability in the following cycles, finally maintains at 948, 889, and \(806~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{h}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at the 300th cycle at current density of 200, 500 and \(1000\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}\) , respectively, indicating significantly improved electrochemical performance for \(\mathrm{CNT/C/Al_{2}(O H)_{2.76}F_{3.24}/S}\) LSBs cathodes due to the incorporation of conductive CNTs and the chemical immobilizing effect for polysulfide intermediates from the \(\mathrm{Al_{2}(O H)_{2.76}F_{3.24}}\) . According to the former reported articles, 2 main reasons for the capacity decay during initial cycles could be concluded. 1) Carbonate materials with high surface area were reported to display high irreversible capacity upon the initial several cycles therefore, using carbonate materials in this work might lead to the initial capacity decay for sulfur containing cathodes. [53] 2) The loss of active materials on the surface was inevitable for the high sulfur content in cathodes by dissolving of polysulfides into the electrolyte during the initial cycles. The capacity for the CNT/MOFs-C/S hybrid shown in Fig. 5b [13] could be easily distinguished with initial discharge capacity of 1281, 1200 and \(1052\;\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , and for the 300th cycled capacity is 769, 682, 614 mA \(\mathrm{~h~g~}^{-1}\) at current density of 200, 500 and \(1000\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , which is much higher than that of the MOFs-C/S cathodes. This illustrates that the twinned CNTs improve the charge transfer kinetics and prevents the polysulfides from dissolution effectively.[54] For comparison, Fig. 5c shows the cycling performance of LSBs based on the MOFs-C/S electrodes under a current density of 200, 500 and \(1000\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}\) between 2.8 and \(1.6~\mathrm{V}.\) The initial discharge capacity for MOFs-C/S electrodes is 1239, 1119, and \(1050\ \mathrm{mA\h\g^{-1}}\) at current density 200, 500 and \(1000\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}\) , respectively. After 300 cycles, the specific capacity are 725, 634, \(725\,\mathrm{\mA\h\g^{-1}}\) , respectively. Specially and notably, it shows excellent long cycling performance for CNTs/MOF- \(\mathrm{C/Al_{2}(O H)_{2.76}F_{3.24}/S}\) LSBs cathodes with a sulfur loading of \(3.1~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-1}\) and \(70~\mathrm{uL}\) electrolyte per cell. A specific capacity of \(719\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) can be maintained at 2000 cycles even at a current density of \(2\,\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}\) , with a coulombic efficiency of approximately \(100\%\) , showing capacity decay only about \(0.02\%\) per cycle (Fig. 5d). The interconnected CNTs greatly facilitate the charge transfer processes, and the \(\mathrm{Al_{2}(O H)_{2.76}F_{3.24}}\) particles could play important role in immobilizing polysulfide intermediates effectively, thus leading to excellent electrochemical performance for CNTs/MOFs- \(\mathrm{C/Al_{2}(O H)_{2.76}F_{3.24}/S}\) hybrid cathode for LSBs.
Charge and discharge voltage profiles based on MOFs-C/S, CNTs/MOFs-C/S and CNTs/MOFs- \(\mathrm{C/Al_{2}(O H)_{2.76}F_{3.24}/S}\) cathodes at 200, 500 and \(1000\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}\) rate within a potential window of 2.8-1.6V versus \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/L i}\) are presented in Fig. 5e, 5f, 5g, respectively. Plateaus in the charge curves stand for the conversion from \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) to \(\mathrm{S}_{8}\) , while plateaus in the discharge curves at \(\sim2.3–2.4\mathrm{~V~}\) represent the reduction of \(\mathrm{S}_{8}\) to high-order lithium polysulfides and to the formation of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) at \(\sim2.0–2.1\mathrm{~V~}\) [85] The specific capacity from the plateau at \(\sim2.3–2.4\mathrm{~V~}\) of discharge curve marked as \(\Delta C\) is about 98, 200, \(220\,\mathrm{\mA\h\g^{-1}}\) for MOFs-C/S, CNTs/MOFs-C/S and CNTs/MOFs- \(\mathrm{C/Al_{2}(O H)_{2.76}F_{3.24}/S}\) cathodes, respectively. The AC of the CNTs/MOFs- \(\mathrm{C/Al_{2}(O H)_{2.76}F_{3.24}/S}\) cathode is much larger and the tails are much smaller than that of the MOFs-C/S electrode, which is a typical signal that polysulfides dissolution is significantly inhibited, polysulfides are immobilized within the cathodes and more sufficient utilization of active materials. [56, 57] The value of potential difference for charge and discharge process marked as \(\Delta\mathrm{E}\) presents the degree for polarization. [58] The presence of CNTs decreases the polarization effectively, demonstrating the important role of the CNTs and \(\mathrm{Al_{2}(O H)_{2.76}F_{3.24}}\) particles for improving electrochemical performance of the \(\mathrm{CNT/C/Al_{2}(O H)_{2.76}F_{3.24}/S}\) hybrid LSBs cathode.
The CV profiles of MOFs-C/S, CNTs/MOFs-C/S and CNTs/MOFs- \(\mathrm{C/Al_{2}(O H)_{2.76}F_{3.24}/S}\) cathodes obtained
between 2.8-1.6 V at a scan rate of \(0.1\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) are depicted in Fig. 6. For the CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S cathode (Fig. 6a), there shows two characteristic reductive peaks at about 2.34 (marked as \(E_1\)) and 2.04 V (marked as \(E_2\)) in the CV curves. \(E_1\) is related to the conversion process from sulfur to long chain \(\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}_n\) (\(4 \leq n \leq 8\)), and \(E_2\) is derived from the reduction of \(\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}_n\) (\(4 \leq n \leq 8\)) to \(\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}_2\) and \(\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}\). [59, 60] For the charged process, two peaks at 2.35 V (\(E_3\)) and at 2.39 V (\(E_4\)) correspond to the oxidation reaction for \(\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}_2\) or \(\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}\) to \(\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}_n\) (\(4 \leq n \leq 8\)) and \(\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}_n\) (\(4 \leq n \leq 8\)) to \(S_8\). [61] The CV curves of the MOFs-S and CNT/MOFs-C/S are very similar to that of the CNT/C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S cathode. In comparison with CV curve of CNT/C-S (Fig. 6b), for the CV peaks of MOFs-C/S cathodes (Fig. 6c), the redox peak position increases from 2.30 to 2.34 V (\(E_1\)), and from 1.93 to 2.03 V (\(E_2\)), while the oxidization peak position decreases from 2.44 to 2.38 V (\(E_3\)). Furthermore, the \(E_3\) and \(E_4\) peaks in Fig. 6b and 6c couldn't be separated clearly, demonstrating that the \(\mathrm{Al}_2(\mathrm{OH})_2.76\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) can effectively reduce the polarization effect. [62] The significant change in CV curves suggests that the electrochemical kinetics are enhanced and the polarization effect is reduced by the interconnected CNTs networks and polarized \(\mathrm{Al}_2(\mathrm{OH})_2.76\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\). [6, 63-64] What's more, the breadth for peaks of MOFs-C/S cathodes are wider than that of CNTs/MOFs-C/S and CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S. It is deduced that sluggish redox kinetics of the cathode is significantly enhanced due to the introduction of interconnected CNTs and \(\mathrm{Al}_2(\mathrm{OH})_2.76\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\).
To further evaluate the chemical immobilizing effect of lithium polysulfides by MOFs-C/S, CNTs/MOFs-C/S and CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S cathodes, visible adsorption measurements are carried out (Fig. 6d). For a comparison study, the LSBs cells based on different cathodes are disassembled at charged state at 2.2 V, for that lithium polysulfides are formed at this voltage. Then the obtained cathodes are immersed into electrolyte for 2 weeks. It is illustrated that CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\) could strongly chemically immobilize adsorb lithium polysulfides, showing the least content of polysulfides dissolved, and displaying the most shallow color. This can be attributed to significantly chemical immobilizing effect of \(\mathrm{Al}_2(\mathrm{OH})_2.76\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) and CNTs. The chemical immobilizing effect of CNT/MOFs-C/S cathode is also obviously improved compared with that of MOFs-C/S cathode.
The electrochemical performance of LSBs based on the CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S cathodes can be comparable with and even superior to the reported performance of different LSBs cathodes, such as oxides,[4, 39, 63] sulfides,[17, 38] carbide,[18] nitride,[16] hydroxide,[15] phosphorene,[6] MOFs,[20] metal particles,[22, 64] which is schematically shown in Fig. 7. At a low current density of \(0.5\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), the CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S cathode almost shows the highest capacity among the mentioned works. More importantly, the CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S hybrid cathode in this work reports the longest cycling life span of 2000 cycles, and specific capacity of 719 mAh \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) can be maintained at 2000 cycles even at a current density of \(2\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) with a coulombic efficiency of approximately 100%. Most of the previously reported LSBs only show short life span within 500 cycles, except for LSBs based on S/CNTs-MXene cathode display long cycle performance with 1200 cycles with a lower capacity at lower current density than the present work. [65] In this work, the reasons for 2000 cycle life span are presented as following: 1) The \(\mathrm{Al}_2(\mathrm{OH})_2.76\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) nanoparticles could chemically anchor the polysulfides in the cathode area, alleviating the corrosion of the lithium anode. 2) The long cycling performance was performed in a thermostat under \(30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which might contribute a lot to the stability of LSBs. 3) Although the CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\)/S cathodes showed ultra-stable performance during long cycling process, fluctuation still exist which could be observed in the partial enlarged details in Fig. S11a and b enlarged from Fig. 5d. It is obviously that the novel kind of CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\) is an excellent sulfur host especially for cycle stability and high specific capacity of LSBs.
The microstructure evolution for 100 cycled lithium anodes after rinsing the cycled Li anode with electrolyte to remove the excess salts is characterized by SEM images shown in Fig. S12. Comparing with smooth and clean surface of fresh lithium anode in Fig. S13, rough surface corresponding to severe corrosion is observed for the 100 cycled lithium anode based on MOFs-C/S cathode (Fig. S12a). A typical EDS spectrum for the cycled Li anode in Fig. S12b corresponding to the cycled Li anode based on MOFs-C/S LSBs cathode, shows the existence of S and C, O, F elements, of which C, O, and F elements are from the electrolyte. The presence of S should be attributed to the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) deposition because of the shuttle effect. It is clearly that with the incorporation of CNTs (Fig. S12c), there is less deposition of dendrite \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and alleviative damage of the lithium anode. It is interesting that for the cycled Li anode corresponding to CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S cathode, only a small amount of dendrite lithium polysulfides are formed (Fig. S12d), demonstrating the conspicuous immobilization effect for polysulfide in the presence of the CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S LSBs cathode. SEM was performed to study the morphology and microstructure of cycled CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S cathodes after 100 cycles. As shown in Fig. S14a and b, the cycled cathodes maintained the initial morphology very well, showing a cuboid porous structure. Fig. S14b clearly discloses the details of structure for cycled CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S species, showing that the CNTs twined on the surface of the MOFs-C and interconnected them. The corresponding elemental mapping for Fig. S14a of C, Al, F and S are shown in Fig S14c-f, indicating the well distribution of these elementals. We deduce that the CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24 could immobilize sulfur effectively and maintain the structural integrity of cathodes.
The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) is used to investigate the charge transfer kinetics of MOFs-C/S, CNTs/MOFs-C/S, and CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S cathodes before cycle (Fig. 8a). Each plot consists of a semicircle and a sloping line before cycle, and the diameter of the semicircle is associated with the charge transfer resistance (Rct). [22] The Rct value for MOFs-C/S, CNTs/MOFs-C/S, and CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S is about 400, 290 and \(160\;\Omega\) , suggesting that CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S displays the smallest charge transfer resistance among these cathodes. The lithium ion diffusion coefficient in the electrodes can be calculated from the plots in the low-frequency region according to the following equation: [66]
\[
\mathrm{Z_{re}=R_{ct}+R_{e}+\sigma\omega^{-1/2}}
\]
\[
\mathrm{D_{Li}=R^{2}T^{2}/(2A^{2}n^{4}F^{4}C_{Li}^{2}\sigma^{2})}
\]
Where D is the lithium ion diffusion coefficient, R is the gas constant, T is the absolute temperature, A is the surface area, n is the number of electrons per molecule oxidized, F is Faraday's constant, C is the concentration, \(\sigma\) is the Warburg factor, and \(\omega\) is the frequency. Fig. 8b shows the relationship between the impedance and the phase angle, demonstrating that the CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S sample displays the smallest Warburg factor, corresponding to the largest lithium ion diffusion coefficient in electrodes. We tested the electrical conductivity of the as prepared MOFs-C/S, CNTs/MOFs-C/S and CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S samples through 4 probe methods at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\) , showing an electronic conductivity of 0.58, 0.76, 0.61 S cm-1, respectively. This shows that coupling with CNTS with MOFs-C/S can greatly improve the electrical conductivity of MOFs-C/S. Although the incorporation of \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) into CNTs/MOFs-C/S may somewhat reduce the electrical conductivity, the electrical conductivity of CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S is still high than that of MOFs-C/S. Furthermore, although the \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) is insulting, the immobilizing effect and limiting dissolution of polysulfide in electrolyte contribute a lot to the electron/charge transfer kinetics of CNTs/MOFs-C/Al2(OH)2.76F3.24/S cathode,
which could be described in detail as following: 1) \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) nanoparticles were formed in-situ from MOFs-derived \(\mathrm{C/Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), presenting well dispersed distribution in carbon matrix. Therefore, the absorption for polysulfides by \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) nanoparticles ensures the uniform distribution on the carbon host and close contact of the insulting sulfur with the host materials. As reported, the uniform distribution of sulfur on conductive host and close contact between sulfur and carbon materials could reduce the resistance of interface electron/charge transfer between them. That is to say, the in-situ formed \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) nanoparticles could contribute a lot to the electron/charge transfer during electrochemical process. [9-11, 64] 2) As is known, fluorine has the strongest electronegativity among all elements and plays an important role in enhancing the charge transfer kinetics. Besides, due to the strong electronegativity of F, the Al-F bonding could absorb the polysulfides by Van der Waals' force from the XPS results (Fig. S10). And the hydroxyl functional groups bond with the polysulfides to form thiosulfate and polythionate complexes (Fig. 4). The shuttle effect was restrained due to the immobilization for polysulfides by \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) nanoparticles. Therefore, the deposition of the insulting product on the electrode was reduced, and electron/charge transfer on interfaces between electrolyte and electrodes were improved greatly by \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) nanoparticles. [67-69] After 5 and 10 cycles, the same electrode CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}/\)S is tested, exhibiting obviously decreased resistance compared with the fresh electrodes. The decrease in the impedance may be due to the chemical interaction between \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) and S species, and rearrangement of the physically stable active material occupying among electrochemically favorable position, implying a closer contact and better coverage between the active material and the hosts.
## Conclusions
A novel architecture CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}\) consisting of CNTs twined porous carbon sponge with highly polar \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) anchored were rationally fabricated by using a Al-based metal organic frameworks as precursors, which is proven to be ultra-stable sulfur immobilizer for cathode materials of LSBs, even exhibiting \(719\mathrm{\mA\,h\,g^{-1}}\) after 2000 cycles under a current density of \(2\mathrm{\A\,g^{-1}}\), with a coulombic efficiency of approximately \(100\%\). The MOF derived carbon sponge not only provides sufficient spaces for sulfur accommodation with high sulfur content due to its large surface area and abundant pore structures, but also enhance the charge transfer processes due to the 3D conductive carbon networks. The highly conductive and flexible CNTs interlinked between MOFs-C further serve as a highway for charge transfer, ensuring fast charge transfer processes during cycling. The in-situ formation of hydroxyhalide polar particles \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2.76}\mathrm{F}_{3.24}\) in carbon sponges further immobilize sulfur species by strong chemical interactions due to the hydroxyl functional groups and Al-F polar bonds. The rationally designed route for CNTs/MOFs-C/Al\(_2\)(OH)\(_{2.76}\)F\(_{3.24}/\)S cathode, opens up a new territory for various energy applications.
